us officinalis), and bitter orange (Citrus aurantium).","why_researchers_care":"Researchers are drawn to α-humulene for its broad biological potential, particularly its role as an anti-inflammatory agent and an insect repellent. The scientific community is actively exploring how this earthy terpene interacts with human cells and whether it can be harnessed for clinical or agricultural use. The strongest evidence so far comes from human trials and human observational studies (Oxford level 2b), which are guiding ongoing laboratory and animal research into its therapeutic properties.","structure_function":[{"claim_id":"anti-inflammatory","statement":"α-Humulene is being studied for its potential to support healthy inflammatory responses.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"This rests on 12 papers, including human observational studies and laboratory research on human cells.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":4},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":4},{"design":"review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":2}],"paper_count":12},{"claim_id":"antimicrobial","statement":"Early work points toward this terpene having properties that may support the body's natural defenses against certain microbes.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"Researchers have published 14 papers on this topic, with the strongest evidence coming from human observational research.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":1},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":3},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":4},{"design":"review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":3}],"paper_count":14},{"claim_id":"insect","statement":"This compound is being studied for its ability to act as an effective insect repellent.","evidence_level":"human_trials","basis":"This rests on 13 papers, including human trials and systematic reviews.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000033","label":"author statement supported by traceable reference"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"systematic_review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":1},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":12}],"paper_count":13},{"claim_id":"pain","statement":"α-Humulene is being explored for its potential to support comfort and pain relief.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"Evidence comes from 3 papers, peaking at the human observational level.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":2},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":1}],"paper_count":3},{"claim_id":"antioxidant","statement":"The molecule may support cellular health by acting as an antioxidant.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"This is based on 6 papers, including human observational and animal studies.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":1},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":1},{"design":"review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":1}],"paper_count":6}],"hypothesized_benefits":[{"claim_id":"cancer","area":"Oncology","hypothesis":"Researchers are exploring whether α-humulene can influence cancer cell growth or survival. If this bears out, it could become a valuable natural compound in oncology research.","evidence_level":"human_observational","paper_count":11,"study_to_test_it":"A randomized, placebo-controlled human clinical trial measuring tumor markers and progression in a specific cancer population over several months.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":7},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":3},{"design":"review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":1}]},{"claim_id":"anxiety","area":"Mental Well-being","hypothesis":"Early animal work points toward a potential role for α-humulene in promoting calm and reducing anxiety, raising the question of whether it might support mental well-being in humans.","evidence_level":"animal","paper_count":1,"study_to_test_it":"A pilot human trial where healthy volunteers receive a standardized α-humulene dose, measuring self-reported anxiety scores and cortisol levels over a four-week period.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":1}]},{"claim_id":"neuroprotection","area":"Brain Health","hypothesis":"Scientists are investigating whether this terpene can protect nerve cells from damage, which would be a thrilling development for supporting long-term brain health.","evidence_level":"animal","paper_count":1,"study_to_test_it":"A Phase 1 clinical trial in older adults with early-stage cognitive decline, measuring neuroinflammatory markers in the blood after daily α-humulene supplementation for six months.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":1}]}],"associated_conditions":[{"condition":"ulcerative colitis","mondo_id":"MONDO:0005101","relationship":"detected in metabolomic studies of"},{"condition":"metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease","mondo_id":"MONDO:0013209","re